  "query_preview": "..\n\nPatchy fog, locally dense in spots, will remain possible through the \nearly morning hours, especially closer to south-central MI where \nupstream observations highlight the region of reduced visibility. \nAny fog development will dissipate quickly after sunrise with \ndaytime heating and boundary-layer mixing. \n\nBroad troughing remains in place across the Great Lakes today with \nrelatively weak large-scale forcing overall. A subtle shortwave is \nprogged to track across southern Michigan during the afternoon and \ncombined with weaker boundary layer convergence, will provide \nsufficient ascent for isolated or scattered shower and thunderstorm \ndevelopment as diurnal instability builds towards 1000 - 1500 J/kg. \nDeep-layer shear remains modest, generally 20-25 knots through 6km, \nwith very weak low-level flow. This will favor isolated or loosely \norganized multicell convection with limited thunderstorm longevity. \nThe severe weather threat remains very low, although steep low-level \nlapse rates and elevated DCAPE values could support a highly \nlocalized strong wet microbursts with any robust development. \n\nIsolated shower chances continue overnight as another shortwave \ntraverses the region, but loss of daytime heating should limit \ncoverage and intensity. \n\nA weak cold front will be the focus for additional shower and \nthunderstorm development tomorrow afternoon and evening. Under a \nsimilar environment with CAPE values around 1000-1300 K/kg and modest\ndeep layer shear, this will again favor disorganized convection and \na very low severe weather threat. Additional shower and thunderstorm \nchances may persist Sunday, but forcing will be much more ill- \ndefined, bringing low confidence for shower chances. \n\nA better signal exists for thunderstorms Monday as strengthening \nsouthern flow across the Plains establishes a more pronounced \nmoisture transport regime. Moisture convergence with the elevated \ninstability brings higher end chances for MCS development across the \nPlains that will be capable of progressing into portions of the Great\nLakes. Uncertainty remains regarding eventual timing, track and \ndegree of maintenance. \n\nTemperatures through the forecast period will remain seasonable with \nhighs around the mid 80s through the weekend, settling into the low \n80s by the midweek period.",
